    "rationale": "Chose NO_FIX. The path starts at the Q pin of cycle_counter (uart_rx.v:170) and ends at a gray write-pointer flop in wptr_full.v:52; the shown block contributes only the launch flop, not combinational delay. RETIME rejected: there is no register interior to the chain - _124902_ and _125646_ are the two endpoints, so no flop exists that could be pushed forward or backward across this logic. RESTRUCTURE rejected: the only combinational expression in my scope is cycle_counter + 1'b1, which sits behind the launch flop and is therefore off the reported path (0 ns of the 0.94 ns); re-encoding the counter (down-count to zero, one-hot) would shorten the downstream decode but changes the observable value of cycle_counter for the comparator and any baud/sample logic reading it, i.e. a functional change. PIPELINE rejected: a register inserted here would delay next_bit and hence the FIFO write enable by one cycle, while the reconvergent rx_valid/rx_data datapath and the wptr/full handshake inside wptr_full would not be re-balanced - that rebalancing logic is entirely outside the given line range. The real cost is cells 1-8 (NOR4/NAND4/OAI22/NOR2/AOI211/AND3/AND3/NAND3, ~0.62 ns of 0.94 ns), a wide equality decode of cycle_counter ANDed with FSM state that produces next_bit; cells 9-13 are just the wptr binary increment plus binary-to-gray XORs (~0.24 ns) and are already minimal. A correct fix is to compare cycle_counter against CYCLES_PER_BIT-1 and register that comparison, so next_bit is a flop output rather than an 8-level decode - zero added latency, since the flag is simply computed one cycle earlier. That comparator is in the combinational section of uart_rx.v above line 170, which I was not shown and cannot edit.",
